ame": "Paris, France", "role": "Headquarters"}, {"name": "Versailles, France", "role": "Historical Treaty Site"}, {"name": "London, United Kingdom", "role": "Major Financial Center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Bank of France", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "bank-of-france"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q806950", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bank_of_France"], "tags": ["France", "central bank", "Europe", "Central Banks & Treasuries", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Bank of France contributed to the historical record of the Early Modern period. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Bank of France played a significant role in shaping modern banking practices and economic policy, influencing both national and international financial systems. Its establishment marked a transition towards a more centralized and regulated economy in Europe, impacting the way financial institutions operate today.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-20T06:02:30Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27862251064", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Central bank of france Headquartered in 1st arrondissement of Paris. Founded 1800.", "newValue": "The Bank of France, established in 1800, emerged as a pivotal institution in the financial landscape of Western Europe, particularly during the tumultuous years following the French Revolution. Founded under the leadership of Napoleon Bonaparte, the bank was intended to stabilize the French economy, restore public confidence in the currency, and manage the national debt. Headquartered in the 1st arrondissement of Paris, it symbolized the shift towards modern state capitalism and the centralization of economic power in the wake of revolutionary upheaval.

Crucially, the Bank of France played a significant role in the economic recovery of France during the 19th century. It facilitated the issuance of banknotes backed by gold reserves, ensuring liquidity in the market and supporting industrial expansion. The bank also implemented innovative banking practices and regulations that set precedents for modern banking systems. By 1848, it had become a model for central banking, influencing the establishment of similar institutions across Europe and beyond, including the Bank of England and the Federal Reserve in the United States.

